# <%= current_page.data.title %>

This page covers two things. The first applies to any AI tool at all. The rest is about AI
coding assistants specifically, which carry their own approvals, training and rules.

## Any AI tool must be approved first

Whatever the tool does, and whether or not it touches code, only use AI tooling that has
been approved for use by MHCLG. The
[AI Register](https://intranet.communities.gov.uk/news/introducing-the-mhclg-ai-register-find-request-and-get-approval-for-the-ai-tools-you-need/)
holds the list of approved tools, and is also where you request approval for something new.

If the tool you want is not on the list, or you are not sure whether your intended use is
covered, check with your team's AI Tooling Lead before using it.

## What is an AI coding assistant?

An AI coding assistant is a tool that uses artificial intelligence to help developers
write, review, debug and navigate code. Rather than replacing developer expertise, it
should work alongside you: suggesting code, explaining unfamiliar logic, helping diagnose
errors, and accelerating repetitive tasks. Well known examples include GitHub Copilot,
Google Gemini for code, and Claude Code. These tools are powered by large language models
(LLMs) trained on large volumes of code and documentation.

## Roles and responsibilities

Three roles are involved in the safe use of AI coding assistants in a team. The AI
Tooling Lead and the Lead Developer can be the same person. Each team should keep a
record of who currently holds each role.

### AI Tooling Lead

Every team using AI coding assistants should have a named AI Tooling Lead. They are
responsible for:

* being the first point of contact for developers on their team with questions about AI
  tooling, access, or appropriate use
* maintaining the record of which developers on the team have completed the required
  training and read this guidance
* submitting and tracking AI Register entries and New Requirements requests in ServiceNow
  for new tools or use cases
* managing access to the tools, such as distributing API keys, and revoking it when
  developers leave the team
* raising material issues or new use cases on the AI Register for the relevant teams to
  review

### Lead Developer

The team's Lead Developer, or equivalent technical lead, is responsible for:

* nominating the AI Tooling Lead for the team, or taking on the role themselves
* supporting the AI Tooling Lead on technical questions about tooling choice and
  integration with the team's development workflow
* ensuring AI related practice is discussed and shared through normal team rituals such as
  code review, retros and tech huddles
* being the escalation point for judgement calls on data classification where the developer
  is unsure

### Individual developers

Every developer using an AI coding assistant is responsible for:

* completing the required training before first use and sending confirmation to their AI
  Tooling Lead
* reading and following this guidance and the MHCLG
  [10 Principles of safe and responsible generative AI](https://mhclg.sharepoint.com/:u:/r/sites/MyIT2/SitePages/10-Principles-of-safe-and-responsible-generative-AI%20for%20technical%20teams.aspx?csf=1&web=1&e=pFhhVQ)
* exercising judgement on what data is and is not shared with the tool, and checking with
  their Lead Developer when unsure
* reviewing and understanding any AI generated code they submit in a pull request. The
  author is accountable for the code, not the reviewer
* raising anything unusual, unexpected, or potentially out of scope with their AI Tooling
  Lead

## Before you use AI coding assistants

Before using any AI coding tool for the first time, you must complete all of the following.

### Required training

Complete every module marked as mandatory in both of these Civil Service Learning courses:

* [Artificial Intelligence: Fundamentals](https://learn.civilservice.gov.uk/courses/3TI2CaGVQ8y8Gx06k-0Qsg)
* [Artificial Intelligence: Understanding AI Ethics](https://learn.civilservice.gov.uk/courses/f7Sf3JPkTQiwYgr2qatDEw)

### Policy and guidance

Read the MHCLG
[10 Principles of safe and responsible generative AI](https://mhclg.sharepoint.com/:u:/r/sites/MyIT2/SitePages/10-Principles-of-safe-and-responsible-generative-AI%20for%20technical%20teams.aspx?csf=1&web=1&e=pFhhVQ).

### Confirm and record

Once complete, send a brief email confirmation to your AI Tooling Lead, confirming you have
read this guidance and completed the required training. This provides a lightweight record
to evidence compliance should any issues arise later.

## What data can I use with an AI coding assistant?

You may only share:

* code classified as OFFICIAL, meaning non-sensitive, and not containing secrets,
  credentials, personally identifiable information (PII), or anything OFFICIAL-SENSITIVE or
  above
* code that is publicly available, for example open source libraries or public repositories

You must never share:

* personally identifiable information (PII)
* secrets, credentials, or API keys
* anything classified as OFFICIAL-SENSITIVE or above
* production data

If you are unsure of the data classification of something you want to share, do not share
it and check with your Lead Developer first.

## What can I use an AI coding assistant for?

All use must follow the MHCLG guidance
[10 Principles of safe and responsible generative AI](https://mhclg.sharepoint.com/:u:/r/sites/MyIT2/SitePages/10-Principles-of-safe-and-responsible-generative-AI%20for%20technical%20teams.aspx?csf=1&web=1&e=pFhhVQ).

Approval is granted per tool and per use case, so check the AI Register entry for the tool
you are using to see what its approval covers. Most coding assistant approvals cover four
main areas. The examples below are illustrative and not exhaustive.

**Writing code**

* generating code to accelerate delivery across features, tests, and boilerplate
* pair programming to work through implementation decisions
* surfacing language and framework specific best practices inline
* experimental or exploratory projects

**Reviewing code**

* assisting reviewers in spotting issues, inconsistencies, and improvements before human
  sign-off
* fine-tuning review behaviour via a config file in the repo

**Debugging code**

* pasting stacktraces to get explanations of failures, likely causes, and suggested fixes
* feeding in logs during incident response to pinpoint issues, redacting any PII manually
  before pasting

**Navigating codebases**

* explaining unfamiliar or legacy code to reduce onboarding and context switching time
* understanding how different parts of the codebase relate to each other

## What is not in scope

Exactly what is in scope depends on the approval for the tool you are using. The following
are unlikely to be covered by a standard coding assistant approval, and would each need
their own request:

* system integrations of any kind, described below
* uses for anything involving OFFICIAL-SENSITIVE data or PII
* use cases materially different from those described above
* automating AI code deployment into production without human review
* use outside of work for MHCLG

If you have a new use case in mind, speak to your AI Tooling Lead in the first instance.

## What do we mean by system integration?

System integration means any programmatic connection between an AI coding assistant and
another system, where the AI tool can read from or act on that system without a developer
reviewing each action at the point it happens.

Integrations broaden the blast radius of any mistake or compromise, so they are unlikely
to be covered by an existing coding assistant approval, and need a separate application to
the AI Register.

What counts as a system integration:

* using an AI tool as part of a product or service, for example calling an AI API from
  application code to power a feature for end users
* running an AI agent against a repository on a schedule or trigger, for example automated
  vulnerability scanning, dependency updates, or code review bots that operate without a
  developer in the loop
* connecting an AI tool to an external service so it can act on your behalf without you
  explicitly approving each action, for example posting to Slack or reading from Jira
  directly rather than the tool suggesting the action to you

What is not a system integration:

* a developer running an AI coding assistant locally on their device, with the developer
  reviewing and accepting each suggestion or action
* copying output from an AI tool and manually pasting it into another system, for example
  into a Jira ticket or a pull request description

If you are planning something that sits between these examples, or you are not sure which
side of the line you are on, speak to your AI Tooling Lead before starting.

## Best practice

AI coding assistants can be powerful productivity tools, but they require critical
engagement to be used effectively and safely. The following practices are strongly
recommended.

**Do not trust output blindly.** AI generated code can look correct but contain subtle bugs,
security issues, or incorrect logic. Always review what is produced before accepting it.

**Review and understand before you accept.** If you cannot explain what the generated code
does, do not use it. You are accountable for everything that goes into a pull request,
regardless of how it was produced.

**Ask it to review its own work.** Prompting the tool to check its output, identify
potential issues, or suggest improvements often surfaces problems before you do.

**Give as much context as possible.** The more detail you provide about the codebase, the
problem, the constraints and the expected behaviour, the better the output will be.

**Use it to learn, not just to receive.** Ask the tool to explain what it has generated and
why. This builds understanding and helps you catch mistakes.

**Verify facts and references.** AI tools can hallucinate, inventing function names, library
versions, or behaviours that do not exist. Always verify against official documentation.

**Keep prompts and context free of sensitive data.** Review the data guidance above before
sharing any code or context with the tool.

**Human review is mandatory.** All AI generated output must be peer reviewed before it is
merged or used. As the developer raising the pull request, you are responsible for reviewing
and understanding any AI generated code before it is submitted, regardless of how it was
generated. The responsibility for validating AI generated code sits with the author, not the
reviewer. At least one additional reviewer must then approve it as part of the standard
[pull request process](/standards/pull-requests.html).

## Ongoing governance

* AI coding assistant usage and expectations will be discussed and shaped through the
  Technology Community of Practice (TCOP)
* release notes and data usage policies of AI coding assistant providers should be
  monitored for material changes: by the issuing team for tooling issued department wide,
  and by the team's AI Tooling Lead where the tool was procured by the team

## Summary checklist

Before using an AI coding assistant for the first time, confirm you have:

* completed every required module in Artificial Intelligence: Fundamentals and Artificial
  Intelligence: Understanding AI Ethics on Civil Service Learning
* read the MHCLG 10 Principles of safe and responsible generative AI
* sent an email confirmation to your AI Tooling Lead
* understood what data can and cannot be shared with the tool
* understood that all AI generated code must be peer reviewed before merging

## How to get access

Access to AI coding assistants requires approval and is not self-serve. Whichever tool you
want, start by speaking to your AI Tooling Lead.

### GitHub Copilot

MHCLG is running a pilot, and access will start rolling out more widely if it succeeds.
Speak to your AI Tooling Lead for the current position and whether you can join.

*This is accurate as of September 2026.*

### Claude Code

Access to Claude Code is available through the
[MHCLG AI Gateway](https://mhclgdigital.blog.gov.uk/2026/09/15/the-mhclg-ai-gateway-enabling-more-effective-and-safer-adoption-of-ai-in-our-digital-services/).
Speak to your AI Tooling Lead about applying.

There is currently no other route to department issued Claude Code access. If your team
has a procurement route of its own, you can pursue access yourself with a new request,
following the same steps as for any other tool below.

*This is accurate as of September 2026.*

### Any other tool

A new tool, or an existing tool used in a new way, needs a request on the AI Register. Your
AI Tooling Lead can submit this on your behalf.

You will also need Cyber approval via a New Requirements request in ServiceNow, as that is
required before the tool can be installed on MHCLG devices. Even if the same tool and the same
use case have already been approved for another team, you may still need to make a new
request for your own team.

Whichever route applies, complete the required training set out above and send confirmation
to your AI Tooling Lead before you start using the tool.

